## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a beginner PCA pipeline in a cross-tenant setting with latency constraints and interpret loadings for cohort definitions. Emphasizes data cleaning, scaling, component retention strategy, and practical mapping from components to business actions.

## Key Concepts
- PCA basics: imputation, scaling, centering
- Global vs per-tenant PCA; explained variance threshold
- Loadings interpretation; cohort definition
- Latency considerations: batch vs streaming; incremental options

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode illustrating preprocessing and PCA call
function buildPCA(data) {
  const imputed = imputeMeanPerFeature(data);
  const scaled = scaleToUnitVariance(imputed);
  // assume PCA is available and returns components/loadings
  const pca = new PCA(scaled, {nComponents: 6});
  return pca.loadings;
}
```

```javascript
// Example interpretation
const top = pcaLoadings[0];
console.log(top); // { sessions_per_week: 0.68, content_views: 0.52, ... }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor stability of the 6 components across weeks?
- How would you adjust if the explained variance dips below the target over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical use of online PCA with drift in a realtime setting, plus realistic preprocessing and maintenance decisions. It checks which components to keep, when to retrain, and how latency shapes choices.

## Key Concepts
- IncrementalPCA for streaming data
- Imputation and scaling in a tight loop
- Explained_variance_ratio for component budgeting
- Drift detection and retraining triggers under latency constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: update loop for streaming features
let pca = new IncrementalPCA({nComponents: k});
for (chunk of stream) {
  let X = imputeAndScale(chunk); //  // mean impute + scale
  pca.partialFit(X);
  let Z = pca.transform(X);
  detector.predict(Z);
  monitorDrift(pca.explainedVarianceRatio_, pca.components_);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you set drift thresholds and trigger retraining?
- How do you handle feature addition/removal over time and maintain a stable k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine nonlinear PCA with streaming data, GPU acceleration, and production latency constraints in a high-throughput setting.

## Key Concepts
- Kernel PCA via random Fourier features
- Incremental SVD in RKHS
- Streaming imputation and normalization
- Drift detection and adaptive component retention
- Translating loadings into governance alerts under latency pressure

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo streaming kernel-PCA core
function processBatch(batch){
  const Z = mapToRF(batch.features); // random Fourier features
  const upd = incrementalSVD(Z, batch.mask);
  const comps = computeLoadings(upd);
  if (driftDetected(comps)) updateComponentPolicy();
  emitAlertsFromLoadings(comps);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate RK feature approximation quality in production?
- How would you handle non-stationary drift with changing feature distributions?

